    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Strengths are given differently by different counts: 4,426 British soldiers by one Canadian government figure, about 7,700 by another count, and 13,390 French troops available in the area, most of whom did not take part.</li><li>The British held their fire until the French column was within about 30 yards (27 m), then fired two heavy volleys, and the attack broke in about an hour.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Montcalm</a> both died of their wounds, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in 1760.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a>'s troops climbed to the plateau at L'Anse-au-Foulon after a French-speaking officer of the 78th Highlanders answered a sentry's challenge in excellent French, and it is not known why Wolfe chose that landing place.</p></aside>
    </section>

