    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">June 1759</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> lands on the Île d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and then at Point Levis, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1759</span><span class="rf-text">About 3,500 British troops fail to land at Beauport and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> withdraws after some 450 casualties</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1759</span><span class="rf-text">British troops land at L'Anse-au-Foulon at night and climb to the plateau</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1759</span><span class="rf-text">The British line stops the French advance outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Montcalm</a> are mortally wounded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1759</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> capitulates and the British take the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1760</span><span class="rf-text">Lévis defeats the British at Sainte-Foy but cannot take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1763</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> cedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham" data-v="537f36be42" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Wolfe's line forms on the plateau</b> Wolfe's army formed a line across the Plains, its right on the bluff above the St Lawrence and its left on a bluff and wood above the St Charles. Canadian militia and native sharpshooters engaged the left flank from the trees and scrub.</li><li><b>Montcalm orders a general advance</b> The French regulars moved against the British line. The British lines held their fire as the French approached.</li><li><b>The British fire two volleys and the French break</b> The French fired two disorganised volleys. The British then fired at close range, marched forward a few paces and fired a second general volley, which sent the attackers into retreat.</li><li><b>The French retreat and the Highlanders pursue</b> British troops fell into a disorganised pursuit of the retreating French. The 78th Fraser Highlanders followed with their swords and met heavy fire from the militia that remained in the trees.</li><li><b>Bougainville's column arrives from the rear</b> Townshend took charge and saw Bougainville's column approaching from the British rear. He formed two battalions from the confused troops and turned them to meet it.</li><li><b>Bougainville retreats and the French cross the St Charles</b> Bougainville did not attack and retreated. The rest of Montcalm's army slipped back across the St Charles, the militia holding the bridge.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-wolfe.jpg" alt="James Wolfe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Wolfe.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Joseph Highmore, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1759 the British prepared a three-pronged attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a> expected to lead 12,000 men but had about 7,000 regular troops, 400 officers and 300 gunners, supported by 49 ships and 140 smaller craft under Vice-Admiral Charles Saunders. He landed on the Île d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> on 28 June, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> had surveyed the channel known as the Traverse, and on the next day his troops landed at Point Levis, where a battery set up in early July nearly levelled the lower town.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Montcalm</a> distributed some 12,000 troops along nine kilometres of redoubts and batteries from the Saint-Charles River to the Montmorency Falls. On 31 July about 3,500 British troops tried to land at Beauport and were fired on in the shallows; Wolfe pulled back after some 450 casualties to Montcalm's 60. For the rest of the summer his troops, with American Rangers, destroyed small French settlements along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a>, an estimated 1,400 stone houses and manors, and many colonists were killed. The effort was probably meant to force Montcalm out of his fortifications, and failed.</p><p>Illness spread through the British camps, and Wolfe, bedridden in August, felt that a new attack was needed by the end of September. In late August he and his brigadiers decided to land upriver of the city, which would cut Montcalm's supply lines to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Plains of Abraham&#8221; cites where it covers Preparations:</p><ul><li>Fred Anderson, Crucible of War: The Seven Years' War and the Fate of Empire in British North America, 1754–1766 (Alfred A. Knopf, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The British were commanded by Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a>, and the numbers vary by source. The Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s website, using the historian André Charbonneau's figures, gives 4,426 British Army soldiers on the Plains with two bronze 6-pounder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. Another count has 7,700 troops, almost all regulars, of whom about 3,300 formed a shallow horseshoe across the Plains, the main firing line roughly one kilometre long. To cover the width Wolfe drew them up two ranks deep rather than three. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> Grenadiers and the 28th, 43rd, 47th, 58th and 78th (Fraser's Highlanders) held the centre, the 15th and two battalions of the 60th the left, the 35th the right flank, the 48th the reserve.</p><p>The French under Louis-Joseph, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Marquis de Montcalm</a> were regulars, militia and Indian allies. Charbonneau's figures are 2,000 men of the French Army, 600 Canadians drafted into regular units and 1,800 militiamen and Indian allies; the French had more cannon, but their number is not known. Another count has 13,390 regulars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troupes-de-la-marine">Troupes de la Marine</a> and militia available in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> City and along the Beauport shore, most of whom did not take part, and about 3,500 soldiers in the attack. The militia were poorly armed with rifles and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonets</a>, and the Indian allies were unfamiliar with European tactics. Montcalm placed militia on the right, the La Sarre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and Béarn regiments in the centre, and the Guyenne and Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> regiments with Montréal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trois-rivieres">Trois-Rivières</a> militia on the left.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the night of 12 September the British landed at L'Anse-au-Foulon, below a cliff 53 m high. Sentries challenged the boats, but a French-speaking officer of the 78th answered in excellent French, and the boats had drifted off course. Twenty-four volunteers under Colonel William Howe climbed the slope and captured the camp of about 100 militia under Captain Vergor, which may have held as few as 40 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Montcalm</a>, surprised, chose to confront <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> directly rather than wait for Bougainville's column.</p><p>The French advanced at about 10 a.m., Montcalm riding a dark horse and waving his sword. The British held their fire until the French were within 30 yards (27 m), and the two armies waited two or three minutes. The French fired two disorganised volleys. The British, who had charged their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> with two balls each, fired a heavy volley, advanced a few paces and fired a second that sent the attackers into retreat. Wolfe, already struck in the wrist, was hit twice and died as the French broke. The 78th Highlanders pursued with swords and took the highest casualties of any British unit. Townshend turned two battalions on Bougainville's column, arriving from Cap Rouge, and Bougainville retreated. Montcalm, wounded in the lower abdomen and thigh during the retreat, reached the city and died early the next morning.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Plains of Abraham&#8221; cites where it covers Battle, First engagements, Main engagement:</p><ul><li>Stuart Reid, Quebec 1759: The Battle That Won Canada (Osprey Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Fred Anderson, Crucible of War: The Seven Years' War and the Fate of Empire in British North America, 1754–1766 (Alfred A. Knopf, 2000)</li><li>Rene Chartrand, Quebec 1759 (Osprey Publishing, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay.jpg" alt="Battle of Quiberon Bay" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay">Battle of Quiberon Bay</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Quiberon_Bay,_20_November_1759_RMG_BHC0397.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Paton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>British casualties were 658 killed or wounded, of them 61 killed and 597 wounded. French casualties were between 644 and 716 killed or wounded, thirteen of them officers, and a further 350 men were taken prisoner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Montcalm</a> was buried in a crater left in the floor of the Ursuline chapel by a British shell.</p><p>Governor de Vaudreuil, who later put the full blame for the rout on Montcalm, abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and the Beauport shore and ordered his forces west to join Bougainville, leaving de Ramezay in command of the garrison. On 18 September de Ramezay, Townshend and Saunders signed the Articles of Capitulation of Quebec. The British navy had to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> soon after, before pack ice closed its mouth.</p><p>In April 1760 Lévis, Montcalm's successor, marched 7,000 troops on Quebec, where scurvy had reduced Murray's garrison to 4,000. On 28 April the French won at Sainte-Foy, with about 850 casualties against 1,100 British, but the British withdrew within the walls and the French lacked the artillery and ammunition to storm them. A British fleet that arrived in mid-May had defeated Lévis' support ships, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay">Quiberon Bay</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> destroyed the French fleet, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> could send no reserve. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> capitulated on 8 September 1760.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Plains of Abraham&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Christopher Lloyd, The Capture of Quebec (B.T. Batsford, 1959)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-knox"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-knox.jpg" alt="John Knox" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knoxbezaicones.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Theodore Beza, Icones (1580), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Some of those present left accounts. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a> of the 43rd Foot wrote in his journal of the British volley and of the cry that the French were running as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> lay dying. Dr Robert Macpherson of the 78th Highlanders wrote three days after the battle that the Highlanders pursued the French to the Sally Port of the town and then suffered heavy losses against Canadians in a village and a wood. Wolfe's last letter, dated HMS Sutherland, 8:30 p.m. on 12 September, and Brigadier-General George Townshend's remark that the general had changed his landing place on some intelligence also survive. Francis Parkman told the story in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Montcalm</a> and Wolfe. In 2017 a cannonball believed to have been fired during the siege was found in the Old City.</p><p>Much is uncertain. Totals for both armies vary between sources, and the number of French guns is not known. It is not known why Wolfe chose Foulon, when the original landing site was further upriver. Wolfe's disdain for his brigadiers and the effects of his illness and opiates have been suggested as influences, and some revisionist historians believe he expected the advanced guard to be repulsed and hoped to die gallantly. Montcalm's decision to attack at once has been regarded as precipitate, since he might have waited for Bougainville, avoided battle or yielded the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle proved a deciding moment in the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> over the fate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, influencing the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> became part of what Britain called the Annus Mirabilis of 1759. The charge of the 78th Highlanders has been called the last known successful Highland Charge in history.</p><p>The war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763, which gave Britain parts of New France, including Canada and the eastern half of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>.</p><p>In 2009 a planned re-enactment of the battle for its 250th anniversary was cancelled after threats of public disorder; leaders of separatist parties had called it a slap in the face for Quebecers of French ancestry. The Moulin à paroles, a recital of 140 texts from Quebec history on the Plains, went ahead without incident, though including the 1970 FLQ Manifesto brought a boycott by federalist politicians. A myth has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">Wolfe</a> singing 'How Stands the Glass Around' the night before; Parkman says he recited Gray's Elegy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 13 September 1759 British troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a>, who had climbed to the Plains of Abraham by night, beat the French and Canadian force of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Marquis de Montcalm</a> in about an hour. Both generals died, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> capitulated on 18 September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1763.</em></p>

